1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,791
Welcome to lectures nine and ten:

2
00:00:02,791 --> 00:00:07,865
bivariate analysis, the relationships between two variables.

3
00:00:07,865 --> 00:00:14,376
We cover correlation, covariance, scatterplots and categorical relationships.

4
00:00:15,176 --> 00:00:19,040
Every pair of features is one of three types.

5
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:27,196
Numerical with numerical, like sales and profit, uses correlation, covariance and scatterplots.

6
00:00:27,196 --> 00:00:35,868
Categorical with categorical, like segment and ship mode, uses cross-tabs, heatmaps and stacked bars.

7
00:00:35,868 --> 00:00:44,024
And categorical with numerical, like category and profit, uses grouped statistics and boxplots.

8
00:00:44,824 --> 00:00:47,702
Why look at two variables together?

9
00:00:47,702 --> 00:00:50,745
Univariate statistics miss the story:

10
00:00:50,745 --> 00:00:56,337
one feature's mean never tells you how it behaves alongside another.

11
00:00:56,337 --> 00:00:58,475
It reveals hidden drivers:

12
00:00:58,475 --> 00:01:06,452
discount alone looks harmless, but discount against profit shows heavy discounting erodes margin.

13
00:01:06,452 --> 00:01:09,412
It uncovers structure in categories.

14
00:01:09,412 --> 00:01:17,800
And it feeds the scientific process, since every does X affect Y question starts as a bivariate check.

15
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:22,676
Covariance asks whether two variables move together.

16
00:01:22,676 --> 00:01:30,670
For every data point, multiply how far X is from its mean by how far Y is from its mean, then average.

17
00:01:30,670 --> 00:01:41,643
Positive covariance means they rise together, negative means one rises as the other falls, and near zero means no consistent linear pattern.

18
00:01:41,643 --> 00:01:52,224
The catch is that covariance is in the product of the two units, like dollars times units, so its size cannot be compared across pairs.

19
00:01:53,024 --> 00:01:55,986
Correlation is the scale-free version.

20
00:01:55,986 --> 00:02:06,352
Divide covariance by the product of the standard deviations and you get r, always between minus one and plus one regardless of units.

21
00:02:06,352 --> 00:02:11,418
That makes coefficients comparable across every pair in a matrix.

22
00:02:11,418 --> 00:02:24,200
Roughly, beyond plus or minus zero point seven is strong, between zero point three and zero point seven is moderate to weak, and near zero means no linear relation.

23
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:30,379
A strong correlation never proves that one variable causes the other.

24
00:02:30,379 --> 00:02:37,864
Both may be driven by a hidden third factor, or the pattern may be a coincidence in this sample.

25
00:02:37,864 --> 00:02:43,867
For example, discount and profit are correlated, but so are sales and profit.

26
00:02:43,867 --> 00:02:49,480
Before acting on a single correlation, check what else might explain it.

27
00:02:50,280 --> 00:02:56,077
Pearson measures straight-line relationships only and is sensitive to outliers.

28
00:02:56,077 --> 00:03:04,957
Spearman ranks the values first, then correlates the ranks, so it captures any monotonic relationship and is more robust.

29
00:03:04,957 --> 00:03:14,424
In the Superstore data, discount versus profit is minus zero point two two by Pearson but minus zero point five four by Spearman:

30
00:03:14,424 --> 00:03:22,056
a handful of heavily discounted, deeply negative-profit orders were flattening the straight-line number.

31
00:03:22,856 --> 00:03:29,314
A scatterplot places every order as one dot, one feature on X and another on Y.

32
00:03:29,314 --> 00:03:36,344
The tilt of the cloud shows direction, and how tightly dots hug a line shows strength.

33
00:03:36,344 --> 00:03:46,808
Unlike a single r value, a scatterplot also reveals outliers, clusters and non-linear shapes that a correlation number can hide.

34
00:03:47,608 --> 00:03:49,910
Here is a gallery of patterns:

35
00:03:49,910 --> 00:04:02,573
positive linear, negative linear, no relationship, curvilinear, a fanning spread where variability grows, known as heteroscedasticity, and an outlier-driven pattern.

36
00:04:02,573 --> 00:04:07,408
The same r can come from very different shapes, so always look.

37
00:04:08,208 --> 00:04:15,838
A correlation matrix stacks the correlation between every pair of numeric features into one table.

38
00:04:15,838 --> 00:04:26,816
A heatmap colours that table, darker green for strong positive and darker red for strong negative, so patterns across many features jump out.

39
00:04:26,816 --> 00:04:31,176
The diagonal is always one, and the matrix is symmetric.

40
00:04:31,976 --> 00:04:41,031
A cross-tab, or contingency table, counts how many records fall into every combination of two categorical features.

41
00:04:41,031 --> 00:04:45,519
It is the categorical equivalent of a correlation matrix.

42
00:04:45,519 --> 00:04:56,385
Raw counts show volume, while row percentages let you compare the ship mode mix within each segment on the same zero to one hundred scale.

43
00:04:56,385 --> 00:05:01,424
Here every segment leans standard class at almost the same rate.

44
00:05:02,224 --> 00:05:08,939
If every row has roughly the same percentage split, the two features do not seem related:

45
00:05:08,939 --> 00:05:12,863
knowing one tells you nothing extra about the other.

46
00:05:12,863 --> 00:05:17,466
If the splits differ noticeably, the features are associated.

47
00:05:17,466 --> 00:05:25,238
Segment versus ship mode lands near sixty percent standard class in every row, so it looks independent.

48
00:05:25,238 --> 00:05:31,576
A visible pattern like this is what later becomes a chi-square test of independence.

49
00:05:32,376 --> 00:05:37,029
For categorical against numerical, there are three tools.

50
00:05:37,029 --> 00:05:39,233
Grouped summary statistics:

51
00:05:39,233 --> 00:05:46,253
split by category, then compute mean, median and standard deviation within each group.

52
00:05:46,253 --> 00:05:47,967
Boxplots by category:

53
00:05:47,967 --> 00:05:53,599
the full distribution per group, showing spread and not just average.

54
00:05:53,599 --> 00:05:59,640
And a bar chart of group means for a quick comparison of central tendency.

55
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:02,945
Which technique for which pair?

56
00:06:02,945 --> 00:06:04,966
Numerical with numerical:

57
00:06:04,966 --> 00:06:12,563
covariance and correlation, scatterplot or pairplot; watch for non-linear shapes and outliers.

58
00:06:12,563 --> 00:06:14,907
Categorical with categorical:

59
00:06:14,907 --> 00:06:23,070
cross-tab counts and percentages, heatmap or stacked bar; watch for rare categories with tiny counts.

60
00:06:23,070 --> 00:06:25,252
Categorical with numerical:

61
00:06:25,252 --> 00:06:32,526
grouped statistics, boxplot or bar chart; with skewed groups the median can beat the mean.

62
00:06:32,526 --> 00:06:38,264
Identify each column's type first, and that tells you which row to use.

63
00:06:39,064 --> 00:06:40,194
Four pitfalls.

64
00:06:40,194 --> 00:06:41,890
Spurious correlation:

65
00:06:41,890 --> 00:06:50,691
unrelated features can correlate by chance, especially in small samples, so ask if it makes real-world sense.

66
00:06:50,691 --> 00:06:52,871
Outlier-driven correlation:

67
00:06:52,871 --> 00:07:00,945
a handful of extreme points can create or hide a correlation, so pair the number with a scatterplot.

68
00:07:00,945 --> 00:07:02,399
Simpson's paradox:

69
00:07:02,399 --> 00:07:07,405
a trend in the whole dataset can reverse within each subgroup.

70
00:07:07,405 --> 00:07:12,976
And small subgroup counts can make a cross-tab percentage misleading.

71
00:07:13,776 --> 00:07:14,990
Key takeaways.

72
00:07:14,990 --> 00:07:18,981
Start by identifying the type of each feature.

73
00:07:18,981 --> 00:07:25,574
Covariance shows direction; correlation rescales it so pairs are comparable.

74
00:07:25,574 --> 00:07:33,641
Pearson catches linear patterns; Spearman catches any monotonic pattern and resists outliers.

75
00:07:33,641 --> 00:07:42,402
A correlation matrix and heatmap summarise all pairs, and scatterplots show what one number can hide.

76
00:07:42,402 --> 00:07:46,740
Cross-tabs are the categorical correlation matrix.

77
00:07:46,740 --> 00:07:49,602
And correlation is not causation:

78
00:07:49,602 --> 00:07:54,460
every finding is the seed of a hypothesis to test later.

79
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:55,848
Next is the lab.

